<body>
    <div class="container">
        <header>
            <h1>Why is the Sky Blue?</h1>
            <p>为什么天空是蓝色的？</p>
        </header>

        <div class="content">
            <div class="column english">
                <h2>English Version</h2>

                <h3>For a 3-Year-Old Toddler</h3>
                <p>The sky is blue because the sun's light bumps into tiny invisible things in the air and makes the blue color bounce around everywhere! It's like when you shine a flashlight through water with a little bit of milk in it - you can see the blue light bouncing around. The blue light likes to play and bounce more than the other colors, so that's why we see blue when we look up!</p>

                <h3>For a High School Teenager</h3>
                <p>The sky appears blue due to a phenomenon called Rayleigh scattering. Sunlight contains all the colors of the visible spectrum, but when it enters Earth's atmosphere, it collides with gas molecules (mostly nitrogen and oxygen). These molecules are much smaller than the wavelength of visible light, causing shorter wavelengths (blue and violet) to scatter more efficiently than longer wavelengths (red, orange, yellow).</p>
                <p>Our eyes are more sensitive to blue than violet, and some violet light gets absorbed by the upper atmosphere, so we perceive the sky as blue. During sunrise and sunset, the light travels through more atmosphere, scattering away the blue light and leaving the longer wavelengths (reds and oranges) to dominate what we see.</p>

                <h3>For a Physics PhD</h3>
                <p>The blue color of the sky results from elastic scattering of electromagnetic radiation by particles much smaller than the wavelength of the incident light, described by Rayleigh scattering theory. The scattering cross-section σ ∝ λ^(-4), meaning shorter wavelengths (blue/violet at ~450nm) scatter approximately 10 times more efficiently than longer wavelengths (red at ~650nm).</p>
                <p>The intensity of scattered light follows I(θ) = I₀(1 + cos²θ)/(2R²) * (8π⁴α²)/(N²λ⁴), where α is the polarizability of scattering particles. While violet light scatters most strongly, three factors contribute to our blue perception: 1) solar irradiance peaks in green-blue wavelengths, 2) human photopic vision has lower sensitivity to violet, and 3) some violet is absorbed by ozone in the stratosphere (Chappuis and Hartley bands).</p>
                <p>The resulting sky radiance follows a characteristic angular distribution, with maximum scattering at 90° from the sun's position, explaining why the sky appears deepest blue at the zenith when the sun is on the horizon.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="column chinese">
                <h2>中文版本</h2>

                <h3>对于3岁的幼儿</h3>
                <p>天空是蓝色的，因为太阳的光撞到了空气中看不见的微小物体，让蓝色到处反弹！这就好像你用手电筒照射牛奶水时 - 你可以看到蓝色光到处反弹。蓝色光比其他颜色更喜欢玩耍和反弹，这就是为什么我们抬头时看到蓝色的原因！</p>

                <h3>对于高中生</h3>
                <p>天空呈现蓝色是由于瑞利散射现象。阳光包含可见光谱的所有颜色，但当它进入地球大气层时，会与气体分子（主要是氮气和氧气）发生碰撞。这些分子比可见光的波长小得多，导致较短波长（蓝色和紫色）比较长波长（红色、橙色、黄色）散射得更有效。</p>
                <p>我们的眼睛对蓝色比紫色更敏感，而且一些紫色光被上层大气吸收，所以我们看到天空是蓝色的。在日出和日落时，光需要穿过更厚的大气层，蓝色光被散射掉，留下较长波长的光（红色和橙色）主导我们看到的景象。</p>

                <h3>对于物理学博士</h3>
                <p>天空的蓝色源于波长远大于散射粒子尺寸的电磁辐射的弹性散射，由瑞利散射理论描述。散射截面σ ∝ λ^(-4)，意味着较短波长（蓝色/紫色约450nm）的散射效率大约是较长波长（红色约650nm）的10倍。</p>
                <p>散射光的强度遵循I(θ) = I₀(1 + cos²θ)/(2R²) * (8π⁴α²)/(N²λ⁴)，其中α是散射粒子的极化率。虽然紫色光散射最强，但三个因素导致我们感知到蓝色：1) 太阳辐射在绿蓝色波长处达到峰值，2) 人眼的光觉视觉对紫色敏感度较低，3) 一些紫色被臭氧层吸收（恰普伊斯和哈特利带）。</p>
                <p>由此产生的天空辐射具有特征角分布，在太阳位置90°方向散射最大，这解释了当太阳位于地平线时，天空在天顶处呈现最深的蓝色。</p>
